 "warnings": [
  "Uniform rho(T) is used. There is no radial rho(T) feedback into J = C/r. The optional correction is off so published numbers stay bit-stable. At 0.5 T and about 26 C this error is small.",
  "Cooling holes are modelled as graded round holes that do not disturb the 1/r profile. This is not a Florida-Bitter plate. Real plates use elongated, staggered holes because round holes concentrate current and hoop stress.",
  "Helical slit and plate-to-plate contact resistance are omitted from this resistance (0.02124 ohm). Contact and busbar resistance can dominate a coil near 1.76 mohm.",
  "Hoop stress bound 0.26 MPa is the thin-ring upper estimate sigma = C*B/lambda. It is far below hard-copper yield. This 0.5 T design is not stress-limited.",
  "No critical heat flux, onset of nucleate boiling, or deionized-water chemistry in the continuum evaluation. A pump-failure time to 85 C is an adiabatic lumped estimate and is invalid once the wall boils.",
  "Swiss-roll SNR gain is a heuristic. It is not an MRI SNR measurement. mu_eff(0) = 1.",
  "Constraints at or near their bounds: homogeneity, R1 lower bound.",
  "No FEniCS model is included. Off-axis checks stay on the loop field and the segment engine.",
  "Existing water correlations, valid roughly 1-95 C. Real Bitter water is deionized (order 1 Mohm cm) and preferably deaerated. Aerated water can mark copper at a few m/s. Deaerated high-purity water is commonly run near 8-15 m/s in high-field labs. This 0.5 T design is far below that.",
  "Stand-in for the 0.25 mm insulator assumption. Continuous rating about 220 C. Dielectric strength is high. Water uptake is a known Bitter-plate failure mode.",
  "Non-current-carrying housing. Used only for a secondary external-convection term and a mass estimate. Conductivity does not create a shorted turn unless conductive bore is enabled.",
  "Housing conductivity is ignored. Enable conductive bore to attach the shorted-turn warning.",
  "Plate-to-plate contact resistance is sampled only in the emulation, not in the continuum resistance.",
  "ONB uses the Bergles-Rohsenow water correlation and is labeled approximate. There is no critical-heat-flux map."
 ],
